How can you modify the following C++ code to optimize its performance by minimizing the number of function calls?
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int factorial(int n) {
    if(n==0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * factorial(n-1);
    }
}
int main() {
    int n;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> n;
    cout << "Factorial of " << n << " is " << factorial(n);
    return 0;
}
```

output:
One way to optimize the performance of the given C++ code is to use memoization. Memoization is an optimization technique that involves storing the results of expensive function calls and returning the cached result when the same inputs occur again.
In this case, we can modify the `factorial` function to store the results of previous calculations in an array, so that we can avoid calculating the factorial of the same number multiple times. Here's the modified code:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
const int MAXN = 1000;
int fact[MAXN];
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    if (fact[n] != 0) {
        return fact[n];
    }
    fact[n] = n * factorial(n-1);
    return fact[n];
}
int main() {
    int n;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> n;
    cout << "Factorial of " << n << " is " << factorial(n) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this modified code, we have created an array `fact` of size `MAXN` to store the factorial values. We have also added a check to see if the factorial of `n` has already been calculated and stored in the array. If it has, we simply return the cached result. Otherwise, we calculate the factorial and store it in the array for future use.
This modification reduces the number of function calls and improves the performance of the program.

