Here is the starting code that takes in the side lengths of a triangle:
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math"
)
func main() {
    var a, b, c float64
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side a: ")
    fmt.Scan(&a)
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side b: ")
    fmt.Scan(&b)
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side c: ")
    fmt.Scan(&c)
    if a+b > c && b+c > a && a+c > b {
        perimeter := a + b + c
        s := perimeter / 2
        area := math.Sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))
        fmt.Printf("Perimeter = %v\n", perimeter)
        fmt.Printf("Area = %v\n", area)
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Not a valid triangle")
    }
}
What changes do I need to make to the code to accurately classify the type of triangle and its angles? Please note that this requires multiple-step reasoning and a thorough understanding of geometric principles.

output:
To classify the type of triangle and its angles, you need to add additional conditional statements to the code after the check for a valid triangle. Here is an updated version of the code that includes these additional statements:
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math"
)
func main() {
    var a, b, c float64
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side a: ")
    fmt.Scan(&a)
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side b: ")
    fmt.Scan(&b)
    fmt.Print("Enter the length of side c: ")
    fmt.Scan(&c)
    if a+b > c && b+c > a && a+c > b {
        perimeter := a + b + c
        s := perimeter / 2
        area := math.Sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))
        fmt.Printf("Perimeter = %v\n", perimeter)
        fmt.Printf("Area = %v\n", area)
        // Check if the triangle is equilateral
        if a == b && b == c {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is equilateral.")
        }
        // Check if the triangle is isosceles
        if a == b || b == c || a == c {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is isosceles.")
        }
        // Check if the triangle is scalene
        if a != b && b != c && a != c {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is scalene.")
        }
        // Check if the triangle is right-angled
        if math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(b, 2) == math.Pow(c, 2) ||
            math.Pow(b, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) == math.Pow(a, 2) ||
            math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) == math.Pow(b, 2) {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is right-angled.")
        }
        // Check if the triangle is acute-angled
        if math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(b, 2) > math.Pow(c, 2) &&
            math.Pow(b, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) > math.Pow(a, 2) &&
            math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) > math.Pow(b, 2) {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is acute-angled.")
        }
        // Check if the triangle is obtuse-angled
        if math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(b, 2) < math.Pow(c, 2) ||
            math.Pow(b, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) < math.Pow(a, 2) ||
            math.Pow(a, 2)+math.Pow(c, 2) < math.Pow(b, 2) {
            fmt.Println("The triangle is obtuse-angled.")
        }
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Not a valid triangle")
    }
}
```
In this updated code, we first check if the triangle is valid using the same conditional statement as before. If the triangle is valid, we then add additional conditional statements to check the type of triangle and its angles.
To check if the triangle is equilateral, we simply check if all three sides are equal. To check if the triangle is isosceles, we check if at least two sides are equal. To check if the triangle is scalene, we check if all three sides are different.
To check the type of angle, we use the Pythagorean theorem. If a^2 + b^2 = c^2, the triangle is right-angled. If a^2 + b^2 < c^2, the triangle is obtuse-angled. If a^2 + b^2 > c^2, the triangle is acute-angled. We check these conditions for all three possible combinations of sides.
With these additional conditional statements, the code is now able to accurately classify the type of triangle and its angles based on its side lengths.

