To improve the performance and accuracy of our nth Fibonacci number generator in Swift, we can incorporate more complex XML data structures such as an XML tree that stores the Fibonacci sequence. This will allow us to easily access any Fibonacci number that we need without having to perform the calculation each time.
Here's an implementation that incorporates an XML tree to generate the nth Fibonacci number:
```
import Foundation
import SWXMLHash
func fibonacci(n: Int) -> Int {
    if n <= 1 {
        return n
    }

    let xml = """
    <fibonacci>
        <number value="0"/>
        <number value="1"/>
    </fibonacci>
    """
    let xmlData = xml.data(using: .utf8)!
    let xmlTree = try! XMLHash.parse(xmlData)

    if let number = xmlTree["fibonacci"]["number"].all.filter({ $0.attributes["value"] == "\(n)" }).first {
        return Int(number.attributes["value"]!)!
    }

    for i in 2...n {
        let previous1 = xmlTree["fibonacci"]["number"].all.filter({ $0.attributes["value"] == "\(i-1)" }).first!
        let previous2 = xmlTree["fibonacci"]["number"].all.filter({ $0.attributes["value"] == "\(i-2)" }).first!
        let sum = Int(previous1.attributes["value"]!)! + Int(previous2.attributes["value"]!)!

        let newNumber = XMLIndexer.Element(name: "number", attributes: ["value": "\(i)", "fibonacci": "\(sum)"])
        xmlTree["fibonacci"].addChild(newNumber)
    }

    return Int(xmlTree["fibonacci"]["number"].all.last!.attributes["value"]!)!
}
print(fibonacci(n: 10))
```
This implementation first checks if the nth Fibonacci number is already stored in the XML tree. If it is, the function returns that number. Otherwise, it calculates the nth Fibonacci number using the previous two numbers in the sequence and adds the new number to the XML tree.
To handle input validation and error handling, we can add some additional checks to the function. For example, we can check if n is less than 0 or greater than a certain maximum value and throw an error if it is. We can also add checks for non-integer inputs and throw an error if the input is not valid.
To optimize the performance of the implementation, we can use a more efficient algorithm for calculating Fibonacci numbers such as matrix exponentiation. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n) instead of the O(n) time complexity of the recursive algorithm used in the previous implementation. Here's an implementation of the matrix exponentiation algorithm:
```
func fibonacci(n: Int) -> Int {
    if n <= 1 {
        return n
    }

    let matrix = [[1, 1], [1, 0]]
    let result = matrixPower(matrix: matrix, power: n-1)

    return result[0][0]
}
func matrixPower(matrix: [[Int]], power: Int) -> [[Int]] {
    if power == 0 {
        return [[1, 0], [0, 1]]
    }

    var result = matrixPower(matrix: matrix, power: power/2)
    result = matrixMultiply(matrix1: result, matrix2: result)

    if power % 2 == 1 {
        result = matrixMultiply(matrix1: result, matrix2: matrix)
    }

    return result
}
func matrixMultiply(matrix1: [[Int]], matrix2: [[Int]]) -> [[Int]] {
    let rows = matrix1.count
    let columns = matrix2[0].count
    let result = Array(repeating: Array(repeating: 0, count: columns), count: rows)

    for i in 0..<rows {
        for j in 0..<columns {
            for k in 0..<matrix2.count {
                result[i][j] += matrix1[i][k] * matrix2[k][j]
            }
        }
    }

    return result
}
print(fibonacci(n: 10))
```
This implementation uses the matrix exponentiation algorithm to calculate the nth Fibonacci number. It first initializes a 2x2 matrix with the values [1, 1], [1, 0]. It then raises this matrix to the power of n-1 using the matrixPower function. Finally, it returns the value at position (0, 0) in the resulting matrix.
The matrixPower function uses recursion to calculate the matrix raised to a certain power. It first checks if the power is 0 and returns the identity matrix if it is. It then recursively calculates the matrix raised to half the power and multiplies the result by itself. If the power is odd, it multiplies the result by the original matrix. This reduces the number of matrix multiplications needed and results in a faster algorithm.
The matrixMultiply function multiplies two matrices together and returns the resulting matrix. It uses three nested loops to perform the multiplication and has a time complexity of O(n^3).
Overall, these improvements should result in a faster and more accurate implementation of the nth Fibonacci number generator in Swift.

