To generate a distinct 5-character alphanumeric string that contains both upper and lowercase letters as well as digits, we can use the following Java code:
```java
import java.security.SecureRandom;
public class UniqueStringGenerator {
    private static final String ALPHABET = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";
    private static final SecureRandom RANDOM = new SecureRandom();
    public static String generateString() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(5);
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            sb.append(ALPHABET.charAt(RANDOM.nextInt(ALPHABET.length())));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
To guarantee the uniqueness of the generated string, we can use a mathematical formula to generate a hash code for each string. We can then store the hash codes in a set to ensure that no duplicates are generated. Here's an updated version of the `generateString()` method:
```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.security.SecureRandom;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;
public class UniqueStringGenerator {
    private static final String ALPHABET = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";
    private static final SecureRandom RANDOM = new SecureRandom();
    public static String generateString() {
        Set<Integer> hashes = new HashSet<>();
        while (true) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(5);
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                sb.append(ALPHABET.charAt(RANDOM.nextInt(ALPHABET.length())));
            }
            String string = sb.toString();
            int hash = getHashCode(string);
            if (!hashes.contains(hash)) {
                hashes.add(hash);
                return string;
            }
        }
    }
    private static int getHashCode(String string) {
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            byte[] hash = md.digest(string.getBytes());
            return hash[0] << 24 | (hash[1] & 0xFF) << 16 | (hash[2] & 0xFF) << 8 | (hash[3] & 0xFF);
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```
This version of the `generateString()` method uses a set to store the hash codes of the generated strings. If a hash code already exists in the set, it means that a collision has occurred, and we generate a new string until we find a unique one.
The `getHashCode()` method uses the SHA-256 algorithm to generate a hash code for a given string. We only use the first four bytes of the hash code to reduce the size of the hash code and improve performance.
